Z||000001||rogergarrison||08-10-2001||05:19 PM||||I thought they should have stormed in and took it back the day it happened. Screw the other countries that wouldnt have approved. Only a few countries give a damn about us anyway. I also think Israilis ought to go in and get rid of the PLO, and while were at it, go in and get rid of Sadam. But thats all wishful hoping. Instead we'll just kiss everyones azz.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||08-10-2001||08:49 PM||||I am another that's tired of the US sucking up to all these (as I heard it put on my favorite talk-radio program) "dog-squeeze" countries. <BR>I also blame Clinton for selling us out to the Chinese. Just my opinion. Unpopular as it's sure to be with some folks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||8
Z||000003||Black88GT||08-10-2001||09:06 PM||||Well for one, remember why the plane had to land in China, it was damaged.  So bad that the pilot was considered a hero for landing it.  So even if we could "storm" the plane, what are we going to do with it?  Can't fly it out.  Can't drag it out.  Also keep in mind we aren't really sure where the plane was, yeah we knew where it landed but who knows where the Chinese stored it for the first week or so.  Secondly, they probably guarded it like it was the holy grail.  I don't care how good our special forces are, these aren't some Arab terrorists with a few hostages.  It would be a suicide mission.  It's not cheap to train a Navy SEAL either (I think I heard 80 grand above a regular seaman) and you would probably have to send in a lot to have a chance (therefore losing a lot).  Also, that would be considered an act of war, we don't want Nuclear holocaust here.<P>It's tough to say what we "should" have done in that situation.  We don't know all the facts, there is sooo much that goes on behind the scenes that the media either doesn't know about, or choses not to report.  We could have done the same thing to a Chinese spy sub, but it was kept secret.  Who knows?  We certainly can't count on the Chinese media to report much of anything, U.S. media is pretty much the same, promoting liberal agenda day in and day out.  So my point is, take a step back and think about all the circumstances or possible circumstances (those of which we will never know about) before we go sending in the troops and starting a World War.<P>Also the "big stick policy" went out the door along with isolationism.  Totally different era's.<BR>      ||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||08-11-2001||12:03 AM||||The proper retaliation would be to send them a computer virus and cripple their economy...<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaRkLoRD||08-11-2001||12:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DJRice:<BR><B>The proper retaliation would be to send them a computer virus and cripple their economy...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They should have just let the chinese gov get the computers on the plane.... when they copied the files over to their systems to analyze them, WindowsNT would crash the whole thing... no need for a virus.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
Z||000006||maryjane||08-11-2001||12:53 AM||||Black88GT, I guess you're right. Pay the sob's their money, we get the crew safely home, the a/c back in pieces, the Chinese get whatever technology they can glean from months of illegal possession, plus the knowledge that nothing bad will come of it next time. I was not suggesting the use of a couple of Seal teams. Seals are the best in the world at what they do but this is not one of their areas. Seal's ops are more highly classified than area 51 so I won't even speculate. They were assigned a  task in Panama, namely, to take an airfield and prevent Noriega's personal jet from taking off. They ran into far superior numbers and fought a good fight but sustained high casualties. The China thing is similar. Within 48 hrs, there were sat pictures of the plane on the tarmac on the island and red cross and U.S. oficials had talked personally to the crew soon after. I'm all for kissing whatever ass as necessary to get our people home safely. As for the a/c, it was on an island! We should have made an airstrike and blown it up ourselves, or used OVERWHELMING force to get it back. <P>U.S.policy has been to keep at least one CNBG(carrier battle group) in the Indian ocean at all times. The 1st Marine Div is already in Westpac. The 101st at Ft Campbell Ky is an air assualt mobile force. Forward units of Marine and Army Divisions are stationed in Okinawa, and Hawaii. B52's are stationed in Guam(last I heard) B-2's can fly non-stop to Westpac (in-flight refueling as they did for Kosovo). Situations like this is why we prepare for 2 MTW's (major theaters of war). We do not have to drain resources from all over the globe to do a little nastiness. Nuclear war with the Chinese? About as likely as Pontiac producing a 2003 Fiero. Continuing to back down is how we got into this isolationist thing to begin with. Of course, I personally wouldn't know anything about military affairs or combat in this NWO<BR>I keep hearing so much about. Black88gt, I'm of course not directing any of this directly at you, I'm just po'd at the sh** we have to swallow. Never did learn to like the taste of it. Good night Chesty, wherever you are.||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||08-11-2001||02:55 AM||||If any of you think there was ever the slightest doubt as to the location of that intelligence aircraft, or that there weren't six different ways of blowing it to very small ashes, including an on-board self destruct, or that any actual sensative gear wasn't unbolted, broken, then tossed into the ocean, then you need to watch Enemy of the State agian, that movie is consevative :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000008||Mach10||08-11-2001||03:59 AM||||It's called diplomacy. Getting into a pissing-match with a country multiples of sizes bigger than yours, with a standing army multiples of sizes bigger than yours is not a healthy activity. I for one would place my money on China, for sheer military strength, but regardless, it would be a long, long war with horrible casualties. I don't really want to go back into that. I've covered it before. The US was caught doing something nasty. **** -hit-fan, it solved itself, everyone went home. China's getting some money out of the deal. Cool. The US has managed to break a deal that results in no-one getting hurt, and no economic sanctions. I applaud that.<P>To clarify, I believe that China is being run by a bunch of slavering, rabid dictators. Communism does not exist in that country. Take from the poor, give to the government is not Communism, neither is shelling university students or trampling over tibet. The fact remains, however, that spy plane should not have been there.||||reg||13
Z||000009||maryjane||08-11-2001||01:55 PM||||Diplomacy? There were two world wars fought in the last century within 30 yrs of each other. Most countries had treaties and ongoing diplomatic relations even as the aggressor nations were unleashing THEIR overwhelming force against them. If a T-72 pulls up to your driveway entrance some day and starts slingin large bore HE at your front door, run out with your white flag & see how far diplomacy will get you. Then get po'd at your govt because they didn't have a clue it could happen since they didn't believe spyplanes and such should be used.  I guess you think we shouldn't have used U2's to detect the missiles in Cuba.The French have excelled in the use of diplomacy, been whipped by nearly every nation that could muster up a few volunteers.(That's how we got Cinco de Mayo) Why do you think the Soviet Union at the height of it's strength didn't make it's promised bid for world domination(we will bury you!-Nikita Kruschev)? You are an educated man Mach10. Think about this  minute. The U.S. has gotten itself in little quarrels all over the world since the end of WW2. Granada, Panama,Kuwait, Bosnia,Vietnam, Korea. No American territory was in question. BUT, no foreign military has set foot on North America either.(Yes, that includes Canada) They realize we will fight, we have the ability & resources to fight, and he**, might<BR>as well admit it, we LIKE to fight. What's the liklihood of Tibet ever getting it's sovereignty back with diplomacy. It's too late for them now. S. Korea, Kuwait, the host of Balkan states too numerous to mention, Panama, and Granada are no longer under unwanted foreign influence. Kuwait & <BR>S. Korea have less than ideal civil rights, but they are autonomous. If you are under 30, you will see in your lifetime another cold war, (maybe a Hot one). This time it will be with a Communist Nation with a rising economy and vast natural resources. Best to keep a close eye on em BEFORE that tank rolls up to your front door. Peace, Mach10 & have a nice day.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>This will be my last entry in this thread, any further correspondence can be by email<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||08-11-2001||03:00 PM||||Fair enough. I'll do some reading, I will get back to you via email. In the meantime, After reading my post, I realize that it was a little poorly worded. I am not striking out against your comment. I am sorry it seems that way. <BR>I'm just happy that this has been solved with no show of arms, damaging economic sanctions, or serious long-term animosity. China was playing the bully here, after catching the US in the act, and the US dealt with it like mature adults. That was the message I wanted to convey. I'll send you an email when I get home.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||08-11-2001||06:15 PM||||I still say we should have did an Entebe type attack to get the men back, got what we wanted with anything on the plane and blew it up there. Let them have the mess to clean up. Ok they have a much bigger army. but what are they gonna do with em, they couldnt get any over here, what would they do pile em all in rubber rafts and rowboats? There Airforce and Navy is a joke. Would prob lose to Argentina. Im not pro Israeli, but at least they dont take crap from anybody, even if there outnumbered. I gained a huge amount of respect for them during the 5 day war with Egypt. They wiped out there Airforce in a day, most of there armor the next. They even did better than we did in the gulf. Im just so sick of the US being run by a bunch of pansy-azz diplomats who are more worried about whos gonna be mad at them at the next official party. No one has respect for us but a few, because we let everyone walk all over us. How thankful is Kiwait for saving there whole friggin country....they should give the US all the oil we want for FREE. If it hadnt been for us, theyd all be hiding in caves and eating lizards and snakes.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tonker||08-11-2001||09:23 PM||||Hmm, interesting thread. I did my B.A in Political Science (concentrating in International Relations and Strategic Studies). It's interesting to see how Americans' view of their military capability compares with that that of the rest of the world. The US is certainly the global hegemon now and I don't think anyone (Saddam aside) is stupid enough to try to start a fight with the States, but it wouldn't be particularly smart to go picking a fight with China in their own back yard, either.<P>China is really the most significant regional power in Asia/Pacific. I don't think the Chinese currently have the technology to compete with the US, but they certainly have the man-power. You really wouldn't want to get into a pissing match with them. It's been a while since I've been doing any research in the Poli Sci field, but my guess is that China is probably going to be the next big power, in fact, I now recall one of my profs saying just that.<P>The matter of who's right in the spy-plane incident isn't really that clear to me, either. I mean, it's acknowledged that it was a spy-plane. Spying isn't really considered a friendly activity. I can't see the US Air Force waving happily at the Chinese if the situation was reversed. Just look at what happens when aircraft from another nation comes gets too close to a US carrier fleet. That's not even the US homeland. Admittedly the Chinese conduct was pretty damn questionable since the plane (I believe) was in international air-space at the time and I'm not trying to defend them here. <P>What I'm really trying to get at is that I'm glad diplomacy solved the problem.  Let's really, <B>really</B> avoid trying to <I>attack</I> China, please?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> I have no interest in seeing a conflict with a nation that has the largest population in the world and the second largest GDP in the world. To quote Dana Carvey doing George Bush (not Dubbya)"Wouldn't be prudent... Not gonna do it..."<P><BR>Some links for those who are interested: <A HREF="http://www.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbook/geos/ch.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cia.gov/cia/publications/factbook/geos/ch.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.defenselink.mil/news/Jun2000/china06222000.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.defenselink.mil/news/Jun2000/china06222000.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.comw.org/cmp/fulltext/iddschina.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.comw.org/cmp/fulltext/iddschina.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.cnn.com/SPECIALS/china.summit/china.threat/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cnn.com/SPECIALS/china.summit/china.threat/</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.cato.org/dailys/03-31-01.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cato.org/dailys/03-31-01.html</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 08-11-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000013||hugh||08-11-2001||10:39 PM||||China probably has more census takers than we have people.Nuf said!||||reg||1
Z||000014||maryjane||08-12-2001||12:36 AM||||Since this has not escalated into a name-calling viscious argument and seeing we have a thread over 20 pgs long that seems to be ok, I will renege and make another reply or two. I have already agreed with Tonker and his proffessor that China is the next likely superpower in the world. There is no question which country could put the most people in uniform, if it came to it. I have not advocated declaring war against China as seems to be the opinion of Tonker,Mach10, and Hugh. The strength of China's ' convent-ional' might is in sheer numbers and  natural resources. Their air capability is marginal at best. Naval forces, less than that.(there is some question regarding the numbers and reliabilty of the sub  force). Any force would be stupid indeed to force a fight on the Chinese mainland. I was referring to action taken on an island. The original statement I make boils down to this. Do we talk or shoot? Where do you draw the line? How close to our front yards does a problem have to come? "If it is across an ocean, none ouf my concern"? A plane of an ally;"none of my concern'? Two provinces or states over,"none of my concern"? Half mile away;still"not concerned"? Your home that your family lives in; NOW it is your concern? Too late then. If you are driving your Fiero and two carjackers want it, do you use deadly force to stop them if you have the means, or just ask them not to?  If the two take the car without any fight put up and you go to driving your second Fiero the next day, what is to stop them from taking it too? Nothing happened the first time so I would think they would believe it's ok from now on. Scenario: The year is 2015. China has been able to do as it wants. It forcibly takes the questioned territories mentioned in the links without action from the U.S., Britian, and allies. They now decide they want the oil from the mid East to fuel their now juggernaut economy, cutting shipping lanes to European continent and North America. Your Fiero sits (if it has been recovered by Police Auto theft Unit) in your drive with no gas. Your families shiver with no heating oil. We in the southern U.S. swelter with no A/C. Is it close enough now? Are you telling me we should not do any spying at all, even in international air space? Do we pick how we defend our freedoms based on a country's size and population? It may be a new millenium, but the threats from the last one are still here. Ignore them if you want, just don't wait so long that they are up close and in your face. War is an ugly, nasty business. You that haven't been there: the news stories don't begin to tell the story. First hand accounts can't relay the horror. Not living free is far worse. I've seen that too. Where will YOU be willing to take a stand? <p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||artherd||08-12-2001||06:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by maryjane:<BR><B>War is an ugly, nasty business... Not living free is far worse.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Amen to that.<P>"It is impossible to enslave a free man, the worst you may do is kill him."  -Robert A Hienlin.<P>(PS, numbers or not, China wouldn't stand a chance in all out conflict.  Just more targets for far better guns on the US side. I still am of the opinion that we should have extracted our soldiers, and blown the plane as soon as it became clear they were beind held against their will.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000016||EdsB52||08-12-2001||08:49 AM||||"I also blame Clinton for selling us out to the Chinese."<P>Didn't this occur during the Bush Administration, or is my calendar off (not sarcastic)? Ya, Clinton did sell us out in that regard, but Bush didn't act with military might with this situation either.<P>"Can't fly it out."<P>Actually we could have with minor repairs, and minimum crew and equipment, the pricks wouldn't let us just to be dicks.<P>"Also the "big stick policy" went out the door along with isolationism. Totally different eras."<P>Such wisdom for such a young guy. At his age I would have said 'nuke 'em.' You’ll make a good airman, just finish college and go to OCS.<P>"The proper retaliation would be to send them a computer virus and cripple their economy..."<P>I say boycott the Chinese finger-cuffs next time you go to the State Fair. Take the Chiclets instead.<P>"B52's are stationed in Guam(last I heard) B-2's can fly non-stop to Westpac (in-flight refueling as they did for Kosovo)."<P>I was stationed there almost 20 years ago. I worked in Phase Dock on B-52D models. Shortly after that they phased-out the "D" models because they are sh!t compared to the "G" and "H" models. I think by now the "H"'s are the only survivors. I heard from someone reliable that Guam no longer has B-52's on it and that the base is vacant but ready for action. I worked on the B-1 in manufacturing and think the B-52 smokes it.<P>"The fact remains, however, that spy plane should not have been there.”<P>I guess that depends upon who’s version of ‘International waters” you subscribe. The Chinese wanted 200 miles I think, whereas we have a NATO (?) driven policy that states much less. I’m not an expert here, but know the essence of the dispute. If someone has the exact parameters of various versions of International waters, please inform us. An old war history teacher of mine said the international waters boundaries were established a few hundred years ago where your coastline was established by how far your cannons could reach. As the range of arms increased, up to and including ICBM’s, the criteria had to be established and that’s where the problem lies.<P>“The U.S. has gotten itself in little quarrels all over the world since the end of WW2.”<P>They call this phenomenon ‘armistice,’ where war isn’t an event, but a continuum. Armistice is the period between wars where tensions build and deplete until an action strikes up and war occurs. The cold war was the most interesting period of armistice in a while. An example of armistice is the period between WWI and WWII where the Japanese ravaged the South Pacific taking all of the islands except Hawaii and Guam, until December 7 and 10 respectively.<P>“How thankful is Kiwait for saving there whole friggin country....they should give the US all the oil we want for FREE.”<P>No SH!T. Instead they squeeze us with the blessings from our government. I feel bad for the families of the fallen 154 (?) political casualties (US servicemen and women). I’ll wave a flag for those heroes. Good job Schwartzkof, Powell, and yes, even Bush Sr for staying the hell out of the way. <P>“Do we pick how we defend our freedoms based on a country's size and population?”<P>I assume rhetorically asked. And of course no, that’s non-criteria. Even though many members think I’m a flaming liberal, they’re wrong. I’m moderate left, however am a staunch supporter of 2nd Amendment issues, as well as 4th and 14th. As I see it, the main issues with the spy-plane incident are this:<BR>1)	What were the International boundaries?<BR>2)	Who initiated contact?<BR>3)	Did the Chinese have the right to hold hostage the American troops (or as the Chinese called them, detainees)?<BR>4)	Did the Chinese have the right to disallow us to fly out the aircraft? If so, what was the rationale?<BR>5)	Anyone remember the Taiwan situation where China warned us not to back them or else around this time? (Side issue that played in the background)<P>The issues weren’t:<BR>1)	The value of the aircraft. It’s a P-3, which is a piece of crap. The crew from what I’ve heard destroyed the avionics. I’ll bet Davis Monthan AFB, Tucson has a butt-load of them (P-3’s) in mothballs.<BR>2)	How much do you owe us for hospitality? The Chinese misdirected the real issue of hostage taking with this misdirection. They tried to play the victim.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Tonker||08-12-2001||12:20 PM||||The point I was making is:<BR>Does the US really want to become involved in a confict against a regional power across the ocean. Not only is China a regional power, but they are a nuclear power.<P>What would the US do if Russian or Chinese spy planes were doing flying off the coast of Hawaii? They would probably be warned off (or worse) with some severe diplomatic message sent to the offending country. Let's look for example at the Cuban Missile Crisis. The US nearly went to war because of nuclear weapons placed in Cuba. Now I think we can agree that this is a pretty terrifying thought. Short and medium range nuclear weapons withing striking distance of the US mainland (and more importantly Canada  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">). Yet there is a precedent. The US had already staged some medium range nuclear weapons withing striking distance of the Soviet Union (I don't recall where, but if someone wants to call me on it, I can go look it up for you). The point is, that the States is often unwilling to tolerate the very actions it inficts on other nations.<P>Regardless of whether or not you're talking about a seal team landing on the island and retaking or destroying the plane or sending a carrier battle group into the area and flattening the island and the Chinese mainland, it's not good politics. Either way you're likely going to end up with a war on your hands. This isn't going to be Saddam and his "mother of all battles", it's war with a country that has aspirations - and sees itself as a great power; it has a nuclear option. <P>If the US attacked the island China would have no choice but to respond with force. You're talking about an attack on Chinese sovereignty. Even if you argue that the Chinese as a people are suffering under a brutal dictatorship, attacking China would be a slap to national pride. Not only that, but it makes the US look like a bully to the international community. I doubt if there would be any support even within NATO for that. Imagine if you will, a Russian Spetsnaz team landing in Hawaii to recover a downed russian Bear aircraft. Would the US sit by and ignore it or would they retaliate? My bet is on the latter. You can't deal with great powers the same way you deal with smaller nations.<P>The other issue, is how many American lives is it worth to you to send the message to China that their conduct is unacceptable? Regardless of the fact that the US would probably win a confrontation with China, the loss of life would be horrible. <B>Horrible</B>. It would make the Gulf war look like a day at the beach and even the Falklands war (yes, I know the US wasn't invlved in that) would be a pale comparison. Think Vietnam... but worse. I don't care that the Chinese have outdated equipment, you're going to suffer casualties. A lot of them. I don't think it's worth it. These are your boys (and girls?) you're sending off to war - your husbands, your fathers and your sons.<P>I'm not going to argue that the Chinese or the US were right or wrong in this incident. I have no way of knowing all the facts. I <B>do</B> know that the US did the right thing be settling it diplomatically. Any military action <I>would</I> have led to a war with China. We haven't seen a war between great powers since WWII, do you really want to see another? As for the US paying for the incident, I'd be surprised if it was a one way deal. Diplomacy is about give and take. I'm sure there was some sort of arrangement with the Chinese government in other areas that we didn't hear about.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Tonker||08-12-2001||12:33 PM||||BTW, I thought I'd just add that I'm really enjoying this thread. You guys make some really good points, without flaming. Looking forward to your rebuttal.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000019||Mach10||08-12-2001||03:15 PM||||I will poke my head up, and stick my neck out (again). This is a neat debate. Let's keep it neat   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I did a little reading, and was interested to find a few articles on the whole incident. Unfortunately, almost all of them are directly from the media. So much for impartial evidence, huh?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>EDIT:<BR>Damnit, I left out the thing that would have made this post relevant to this topic. The payout. Sorry! From what I could find, The US agreed to reimburse the expenses incurred by the chinese for storing the plane. The recent "bill" sent to you was laughed at. It's fair that the US should pay for the costs of landing the damaged craft, especially if the rest of the situation is ignored. It's not another Defeat. Restituition should have nothing to do with anything other than the fact that you took up space, the crew ate stuff, and some misc. fees for services etc. $1M USD is, er, excessive. To say the least. Pay China what is owed, not what is wanted.<BR>There, I'm done  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Firstly, I take back my statement on the US plane intruding on China's Airspace. All of the articles I've read consistantly show that the plane was in international waters. However, the plane WAS spying on China. Now, espionage IS invaluable, both for security measures, and tactical. Although there aren't any major conflicts that the US is presently in, it does not pay to be asleep at the helm. Having said that, spying is considered rude by whoever is being peeped at, so a reaction when one is actually caught spying should be expected. China was justified in not being pleased. They had justification to send up the jets, if nothing else, simply to "keep an eye on things."<P>So far, I'm blaming the US for incident? No. I can't believe that the Chinese expected anyone to swallow the ridiculous notion that a huge PROP PLANE willfully rammed a lighter, faster, better armed, more manouverable jet fighter. Bullshit. That plane can't handle for **** . It was the Chinese pilot's hotdog attitude that killed him. Unfortunate, yes. US responsibiliy? No. <P>Unfortunately, the plane was damaged severely, and had to do the emergency landing thing. This meant going into Chinese territories without permission. This *is* a faux-pas, but in emergencies, would normally be overlooked. However, the Chinese are still pissed at being spied on, losing one of their jets, Not even taking out the plane, AND having these unwelcome Americans parked on their shores. Understandibly, they are pissed.<P>Now at this point, people are starting to draw parrallels to the Japanese incident where that US sub sunk that fishing boat. Suddenly, it's the US against Asians in general. China figures that this is a good time to try and get something out of this incident. What better way to come out on top than making the US admit responsibility? Both sides blame the other. The US military will not tell us what exactly transpired. Neither will China. All we have to go on is the Official statements. Unfortunately, we have to take these at face value.<P>So, we have two major powers butting heads over an accident. This is normally a BAD THING. The fact remains, though, that this WAS an accident. Maybe both planes zigged, when one should have Zagged. Who knows? If anything, the blame rests on the pilots. It was an accident. I'll bet my car on the fact that neither wanted to run into each other. In my opinion, the blame rests on the chinese pilot.  He had the more manouverable plane, and common sense would dictate that you yeild to the big lumbering cow. Regardless, there was a collision. It happened. Unfortunately, China chose to adopt the schoolyard bully stance. The goal here was plainly to make China look good and to make the US look bad. The US at first reciprocated this, by refusing to admit responsibility. Eventually, however, they appologized for the incident, showing the world that they were more concerned about the welfare of their people on the plane. I don't know why everyone says that they wimped out. China now looks like a bunch of spoilt children, and the US has their plane and people back. What's bad about that? If needed, there's no doubt that the US could have taken the plane if they wanted. There would be SERIOUS repercussions with such a move, but they'd get their stuff back. China didn't want a conflict, they wanted a publicity stunt. The Pilot was a martyr. Why else would the Chinese refuse US help in finding him? China's plan failed. Everyone's scratching their heads wondering what the hell these people were thinking, and the US has what they wanted.<P>So that's my opinion on the incident. The US was in the right, based on all evidence that have seen. <P>Now, on the flip side, I get to see just how the people reacted to this. People cried foul left and right. Spoke of armed conflict to tech 'em a lesson. This kind of behavior makes me sick. First of all, is a stupid incident like this worth the lives of hundreds, thousands of soldiers on both sides? Maybe going in and taking the planes would only result in a minor scuffle and some cold shoulders. Maybe it would escalate into a full-blown confrontation. All of those lives wasted.<BR>Let's look at the repercussions at home, shall we? Feel free to peruse this link:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.80-20initiative.net/discspyplane.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.80-20initiative.net/discspyplane.html</A> <BR>This is the reason I jump into these discussions. It makes me sick. <BR>To finish, to all those people stating that China has little or no military strength, and that they do not have the advanced technology for really bad weapons, why not read the Cox report released by your own government. Find it here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fas.org/spp/starwars/congress/1999_r/cox/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fas.org/spp/starwars/congress/1999_r/cox/</A> <P>In the meantime, I'd like to ask that before suggesting military action against another nation, remember that 9/10 casualties in a war are civilian. Bear in mind also, that China conscripts it's soldiers, and that most of these men will be leaving home to defend thier countries against their will. Also remember that there will be losses on both sides. Your people will get hurt also. Thanks!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Black88GT||08-12-2001||04:18 PM||||I am a little backed up but I will try to respond to what I can.<P>maryjane, part of the reason the storming of that air base didn't work was because they used SEALs.  They all admit now they should have used Rangers.  Also, the only advantage a few SEAL teams have is stealth and surprise.  They had neither in that attack.<BR>Mind you, as I mentioned before, the Chinese are not terrorists or half trained nimrods.  Underestimating your enemy is the first step on the trail of defeat.  <P>Secondly maryjane, how do you or I know exactly what technology they got?  It is well known that most of the secret computer technology aboard has hatches where the crew is trained to throw grenades in to.  We will never know exactly what the Chinese got.  BTW the plane itself is no modern marvel so no big worries there.  <P>Just because the plane was on an island means nothing.  It's still a Chinese island.  If the Chinese were to mount an attack on Hawaii, do you think our gov't would do anything less then if an attack was mounted on California?  It's still our sovereign territory, just like it was theirs.  <P>Also, I think it was Mach10 that pointed it out, this was a SPY PLANE.  I'm not saying that it wasn't the Chinese fault, it was, but its not like this was a U.S. Airways passenger plane.  And if you think the Chinese don't have spy planes and subs, then you should think again.  Spying is an important way for us to evaluate the enemy, it's definitely needed.  But when we get caught or something like this happens, we can't be surprised that the Chinese took full advantage.<P>My point in my first response that we don't know what goes on with all this global espionage was proven in a recent History Channel show called "Blind Mans Bluff".  We used to use our subs to tap underground communication cables in Russian waters.  Russia lost a sub, which we tried to recover, unsuccessfully.  The Russians used spies in the Navy to find out technical info. about our subs, which almost gave them the upper hand in the event of a war.  I bet this is just the tip of the iceburg.  Who knows what really goes on behind the scenes. <P>maryjane, your starting to lose me with all this "tank rolling on your door" stuff.  That sounds like something from a midwestern militia leader. <P>rogergarrison, I agree the numbers of the Chinese don't mean much to us since they are so far.  But they do mean a lot to the rest of Asia.  Also, who do you think gave Israel all their advanced weapons?  It wasn't the Arabs.  Why do you think Israel doesn't take any crap from anybody?  They have nuclear weapons.  Who gave them those?  Same people who gave them most of their weapons.  They did a better job then we did in the gulf? They were 2 different scenario's, apples and oranges.  If Kuwait gave us oil for free, what economy would they have?  They would be better off living in caves eating snakes and lizards.  <P>General Comments:<P>The next war won't really matter who has the most people.  What will matter is who has the most trained military personnel.  Liberals seem to think we don't need to big of a military.  Well they are dead wrong.  Deterrence is our greatest weapon.  If we ever lose that edge, God help us.  <P>Tonker, I agree with most of what you said.  To clear things up a bit, it was Turkey where we had some nuclear missiles, (Jupiter) and part of the deal was that we would remove our missiles from Turkey and the Soviet Union would remove their missiles from Cuba.  BUT!  JFK had already ordered the removal of these obsolete Jupiter missiles a year or two before the CMC.  During the Cold War we played spy games with the Soviet Union on a daily basis.  They would brush our subs with their subs, we would tap their cables, they would use spies, we would do the same etc. etc.  I am sure the Chinese are doing the same thing, while it may be on a smaller scale.  Point being, it's a two way street.  I don't think the U.S. is shocked by what the Chinese did.  While the media may make it out to be that way, I doubt it was the case.  <P>I think Tonker said it best with his last 4 paragraphs.  An act of force would have surely created more trouble then its worth.  Just because our American pride was slightly damaged in this situation, it's not worth a World War.  <P>There was a similiar incident during WWII.  Remember the Doolittle raid?  It was featured at the end of the recent "Pearl Harbor" movie.  Well each B-25 pilot was give strict orders NOT to land in Russia no matter what.  One of them did.  Russia kept the plane forever, the pilots didn't get out until 16 months later (and they weren't technically released either).  They even duplicated the plane down to each nut and bolt!  Where was the outrage then to go attack Russia?  Ironically enough, they were told to land in China.  Just food for thought.<P>  ||||reg||2
Z||000021||maryjane||08-12-2001||05:45 PM||||Tonker, the precedece you were referring to was The Jupiters stationed in Turkey. <BR>Mach10, I assume you posted the link to the Cox Report to show the ability of China to reach the N. Amer. mainland with nuclear weapons. I had assumed this was common knowledge, so didn't mention it. Mutually Assured Destruction has been both a threat and a safeguard to planet Earth since the early '60's. It's presence is why I said"Nuclear war with China? About as likely as Pont producing a 2003 Fiero". But guys, go to that link, read at least the overview and tell me how the U.S. can be cast in such a bad light for their spy activities, yet China is not. Most developed nations spy on one another.*** Fact of life. Even our proffessed friends do it to us. That aside, I guess this incident worked out as well as can be expected. One pilot dead/missing,2 a/c lost, (the spy plane may be repaired, but only as a matter of principle), about $40K paid in extortion money, both sides gain and lose a little face. The easy questions are answered. Now on to the difficult: No one from opposing view  addressed the two hypothetical  questions concerning the stolen Fiero and the oil grab by the Chinese. Where would you say enough is enough? One plane & crew? 2? ten? Our property/lives or is someone elses just as precious? C'mon, I backed off from my call to arms, stick your necks out as Mach10 put it. These questions are easy to ignore here in OT, but our Govts and their Joint Cheifs have to struggle with this all the time. Where would YOU draw the line?*** No offense intended towards anyone)<BR>***=I would appreciate an answer to these questions.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||rogergarrison||08-12-2001||05:47 PM||||kuwait had NO economy if Sadam kept it. We only get a small percentage of what they produce. They would still have a huge national income if the US didnt pay. Have you ever seen how Kuwaiti citizens live. the government there gives everyone free medical expenses, free college tuition, its even been joked on that they throw away new cars when they run out of gas. No one works common labor, they hire people from other countries. But this is all a little off topic. Oh and yes, we give Israel a lot, but a lot of their weaponry is French, English and German too...whats your point.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mach10||08-12-2001||06:14 PM||||Good point MJ. <P>My reference to the Cox report wasn't so much intended to show that China was a super-power, but that they have a very active manufacturing, R&D, and espionage industry, and that they get a lot of their stuff from the US. I.E., they aren't that far behind.<P>As for your questions, I've given them some thought...<P>[qoute]tell me how the U.S. can be cast in such a bad light for their spy activities, yet China is not...[/quote]<P>Excellent point. My position on this is that there's nothing wrong with espionage in general. My previous comments don't really reflect that. Sorry. However, like I said, spying is considered at least rude. The idea here is that spying is ok, if you aren't caught. If you are, expect consequences. Spies are tried for treason if they're caught. In this case, the crew was detained, and the aircraft detained. <BR>I assure you, if a similar situation happened on this side of the ocean, Everyone would be up in arms against China, the same way that the Chinese were up in arms at the US... It all boils down to who was caught for what.<P>I read over the posts a few times, and I am a little confused about your second question. I get the part about the oil, but the stolen Fiero? I've read this topic twice, and I can't make sense of it. I'm probably missing something. Rephrase? I'll try and answer. This is fun, now that people are calming down   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Oh, and for some reason, my edit bumped to the top of my last post. Don't know why. Everything from the "Edit:" to the "  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">" should be at the end. Makes a little more sense.<P><BR>EDIT: <BR>Oh, sorry, I found the reference. Give me a few minutes to ponder it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000024||Mach10||08-12-2001||06:41 PM||||Ok, I'll try and answer the second question:<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Do we talk or shoot? Where do you draw the line? How close to our front yards does a problem have to come? "If it is across an ocean, none ouf my concern"? A plane of an ally;"none of my concern'? Two provinces or states over,"none of my concern"? Half mile away;still"not concerned"? Your home that your family lives in; NOW it is your concern? Too late then.<BR>If you are driving your Fiero and two carjackers want it, do you use deadly force to stop them if you have the means, or just ask them not to? If the two take the car without any fight put up and you go to driving your second Fiero the next day, what is to stop them from taking it too? Nothing happened the first time so I would think they would believe it's ok from now on.<P>Scenario: The year is 2015. China has been able to do as it wants. It forcibly takes the questioned territories mentioned in the links without action from the U.S., Britian, and allies. They now decide they want the oil from the mid East to fuel their now juggernaut economy, cutting shipping lanes to European continent and North America. Your Fiero sits (if it has been recovered by Police Auto theft Unit) in your drive with no gas. Your families shiver with no heating oil. We in the southern U.S. swelter with no A/C. Is it close enough now? Are you telling me we should not do any spying at all, even in international air space? Do we pick how we defend our freedoms based on a country's size and population? It may be a new millenium, but the threats from the last one are still here. Ignore them if you want, just don't wait so long that they are up close and in your face. War is an ugly, nasty business. You that haven't been there: the news stories don't begin to tell the story. First hand accounts can't relay the horror. Not living free is far worse. I've seen that too. Where will YOU be willing to take a stand?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Again, Excellent point. I missed it the first time. Sorry. If this question leveled at me, personally, then my stance is to let them take the damn car. I don't think that a material possession is worth my life. I hope to God that they get caught. I let the criminal code provide the deterrant. True, it doesn't work so well. That's why I need a car-alarm. However, in a civilized society, this is the only thing I can have. I don't believe in possessing firearms (although I have a healthy collection of Medieval weaponry. Any night prowler will be treated to seein me in my boxers, howling, swinging a 5' european 2-edged broadsword) so I wouldn't return fire. <BR>Now you've gone and raised a sticky point. Where do I draw the line? How far do you have to push me before I shove back? I would say personal danger and/or danger to my family. Would I kill to protect them? I don't know, but I would not be concerned with their (the agressors) well-being. <P>With the case of this, I don't believe that there is much threat. The Chinese didn't gain anything from this. In terms of international politics, all the allies were not screaming for China to release the plane. I don't believe China did anything illegal (I could be wrong, but there wasn't much outcry) by holding the plane. I think both sides know that it could have been much worse. For example, China COULD have imprisoned the crew, tried them for espionage, then executed them, and kept the plane. On the other hand, the US does have the means to take back the plane and the crew by force. <BR>The problem is, both of these actions would have had serious implications.<P>Personally, I felt that the US did very well in this situation. They refused to be bullied, and were willing to acknowledge their role in the pilots death. There was no show of weakness. China's newspapers can cackle and gloat all they want. The rest of the world knows who came out on top. The US has shown maturity and grace, while China looks like a school yard bully, egg on his face, with pennies instead of lunch money.<P>Does that cover it? <P>||||reg||10
Z||000025||maryjane||08-12-2001||06:54 PM||||Ok, there were posts and editing while I was typing my last one. Black88gt,I didn't mean to repeat your info on the Jupiters.<BR>My Fiero question wasn't directed at anyone in particular, I try not to get or take anything here personal.<BR>To clear up my awkward questions: I have seen numerous times in the last 35 years, a problem would arise somewhere in the world and the U.S. would get involved. Some immediatly cry "It's not our affair, don't send our troops in" The same people who cry this may be willing to fight for their freedom, or their family's but not for someone's accross the globe. If I were able to ask the President what criteria is used to decide when military force is used, I would just get a bunch of political sidestepping. So, I'm asking each of you, how close to home does a problem in the world<BR>have to hit before you say "no appeasement,fight back". Sorry if I sounded like I was a right wing fanaticical warmonger. I hate war & killing, but know it is sometimes a necessary evil.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 08-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mach10||08-12-2001||07:14 PM||||Oooooooh, that's deep! Well put, even as a rhetorical question! <P>However, I hate rhetoric :evil: I am bound by honor to answer! Mwa-hahahaha!<P>Criteria?<BR>1) Direct, or indirect attack on a country's land, or territory<BR>2) Direct, or indirect attack on an ally/friendly nation, and the said party asks for help.<BR>3) To stop genocide, or acts of Genocide<BR>4) Halt expansionistic growth into smaller nations.<BR>5) Calling my mamma a whore.||||reg||11
Z||000027||Mach10||08-12-2001||08:07 PM||||You know, I plain forgot to read that page. I had it open, and unread for the past few hours. I will decline comment on it, however, because there is nothing I can say that won't  get me in trouble with somebody. <BR>I will say that the article was fairly well writen, if a little sardonic. The point rests, though. Just bear in mind that this does not represent the entire military system. There is nothing like this up in Canada, although there are a few misconduct and harrassment suits pending against some of the top brass in our Military.||||reg||11
Z||000028||maryjane||08-12-2001||09:01 PM||||Yep, know what ya mean. He's pretty much an outspoken guy, I'll probably delete the link.<BR>It was in yesterday's paper here, filler I guess. Not much happens in San Angelo. It's been enlightening. You can start the next one.||||reg||1
Z||000029||Mach10||08-12-2001||09:08 PM||||Nah, leave the link. The guy's a prick (from his tone) but the message is valid. Again, I ain't touching that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000030||Tonker||08-13-2001||12:09 AM||||I just briefly perused the responses and maryjane, you've asked some good questions, to which I'm probably not going to be able to do justice tonight before heading off, but here's a try.<P>To start with, I'm going to admit that my opinions fit in more closely with the Realist or Power-Politics school of though than the Idealist school of thought.<P>In the question of espionnage to me isn't about right or wrong. It's a fact of life and a requirement of the state to obtain timely and factual information about other states' capabilities and intentions. I'm pretty certain that both Canada and the US perform espionnage at some level against one another. It's simply prudent to do so. I know for certain that China relies on espionnage of the US. I doubt that they have the equipment and facilities to send spy planes, but they definately have other means at their disposal, as do the States.<P>I'm going to refrain from critiquing US foreign policy of the past 35 years because I don't currently have the experience or the research materiel required to do so. You did, however, ask when I would endorse the use of force. <BR>When it comes down to it, the use of force is the basis of politics. The ability to bring force to bear is basically where your authority comes from. I don't obey laws because they exist. I obey laws because at some level the state coerces me to obey them - sometimes, often even, they happen to coincide with my own beliefs but it would be naive to believe that the average citizen obeys the laws simply because they are there. If there were no means to enforce my compliance, how likely would I be to pay taxes or obey the speed limit?<P>International politics works in much the same way, although there is no central authority to enforce compliance. Because of this, individual states are still the main actor at this level. I endorse the use of force then, when it is in the best interests of the state. This probably is not going to be an acceptable answer, so I'll try to elaborate.<P>If you accept what I said earlier to be true, i.e that force is the basis of politics( and you're free to disagree with me here), then the threat of force is implicit in any relations between states. Capability then becomes a significant issue. Obviously it is in the best interest of the state, where possible, to resolve issues without resorting to force since any use of force usually has an associated cost in terms of resources(man-power, goods, capital, time). However, there will be occasions where the benefits of the use of force will out-weigh the costs. <P>As an example, let's look at the Gulf War, since it was previously brought up. If Kuwait had no oil, but was instead the world's leading source of salted pig lips, would the US have gone to war with Iraq? My personal opinion is that the US viewed the Iraqi invasion of Kuwait as a threat to its oil supply and took action to ensure that it would have continued access at reasonable prices. I understand and support the use of force in this case. The confontation with Iraq was hardly risky and lack of action might have resulted in destabilizing of global oil prices.<P>If you then look at the situation with China, where you have a military aricraft engaged in espionnage or reconaissance - call it what you will - being forced to make an emergency landing, I don't think we're looking at the same stakes at all. First of all China's military capability is many orders of magnitude higher than Iraq's. The US is not going to be able to simply march in and and recover the aircraft. The costs involved in that conflict would likely be much larger. What is the cost of one aircraft or "storage fees" and temporary detention of air-force personnal against the cost of potential war with China? In addition you're dealing with a state that is growing. It is basically in the position of the US in the 19th and early 20th Centuries. It is poised to become the next superpower. <P>Military force is one tool in the state's arsenal to leverage itself in the international arena. It should be used where it benefits the state - that is, it should only be used where the other means at the state's disposal are not effective at achieveing the desired result. I think in the case of the spy plane. the cost of use of force would have been far more expensive in terms of lives lost, equipment costs and economic damage to the US than the cost of seeking a diplomatic resolution to this conflict.<P>In the case where somebody wants my Fiero, I'm going to do the same thing. I'm going to evaluate my own capability vs theirs. Do they have weapons? Do I? How many of the are there? Am I alone or do I have any allies to assist me?Can I convince them instead to take my friend's MR2? This in fact is possibly a good example. <P>I live in an apartment complex. It's rather low rent. I have recently bought myself a car alarm and "The Club". DO I think that by spending $300 for an alarm and and extra $50 or so for the club that my car can't be stolen? No, it is a cost benefit analysis. My insurance deductible is $300. Every time someone breaks into my car I am out of pocket $300 dollars plus any increase in my insurance rates. What I'm seeking to do is provide myself with deterrence. I want to make myself a less appealing target. If a thief know sthat they have to break into my car and get past my club while my car is screaming bloody murder, maybe they will instead to after the RX-7 a few cars down that has no alarm. The cost up front for me is much less than the cost of being broken into multiple times. The relative cost to the thief of invading my car, since it will be more difficult (hopefully) means that he will look for an easier target.<P>I'm afraid that's the best answer I can give you at the moment. Distance, to me, is not the factor here. It is the relative cost vs benefit to the state(or fiero owner) that determines action. Sorry if this got really stupid.. soo tired.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000031||artherd||08-13-2001||08:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B><BR>...finger-cuffs...<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Heh, just had a 'Chasing Amy' moment there, thanks Ed :)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000032||AusFiero||08-13-2001||08:49 AM||||Well I have read all of this...agree with some and not with others. But from my point of view, Australia is closer to China than you guys. Indonesia, the real dangerous Asians, would back them. Indonesia can be reached from Australia in a small boat. Australia has many US bases and some of the worlds major satelitte communications dishes, owned by us and many by the USA military. Don' start war with them. They will take out our country before yours just to prove a point. Europe would be next. They would wait for the USA to try defend the world on their own ground, not US soil. Who would win then?<P>Yes it was wrong to ram a plane and make it land then play the badly done by. But not worth world war.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000033||Tonker||08-13-2001||09:19 AM||||Don't worry Aus! Canada will protect you! We'll send our, uh... our... Prime Minister.. Yeah!! He's a pretty nasty guy. And we'll drop our our Sea King helicopters on them... if we can get them airborne in the first place. Hmm.. maybe you guys are on your own after all... Sorry Aus.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Our Prime Minister.. do not F* with us:   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/TelevisionCity/6160/chretien.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/TelevisionCity/6160/chretien.html</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 08-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000034||SteveJ||08-13-2001||09:35 PM||||Your driving down the road in another state when a local smashes into your car in an intersection killing himself. The local sheriff sees the accident as your fault and takes you into custody but the judge puts you out on bail until tomorrow's hearing. Your totaled car is towed away to the impound.<P>Now do you expect not to pay the motel bill and not to pay the impound fees?<P>I say the state department got it right even if the whole affair pisses me off.||||reg||1
Z||000035||maryjane||08-13-2001||11:06 PM||||Thanks for all who replied to my questions. I asked the same things around locally plus posted the original statement and subsequent<BR>questions on a Vietnam Veterans forum that I belong to. I've come up with a few observations:<BR>1. about 90% non vets are pretty afraid of war with China. Almost the reverse with vets.<BR>2. Most place the majority of blame on China, a lot fault the Chinese pilot's irresponsible flying, not necessarily the Chinese govt. Most view spying as part of life nowdays.<BR>3. Heard or read several times "old men (I'm 52) get us into wars, the young have to fight & die in them.<BR>4. Most would favor armed intervention if a friendly country was invaded, but it would depend on what else was threatened, but it also depended on which country it was.Canada,Mexico,Great Britian,Austrailia and New Zealand were mentioned as "defendable allies" . France & Tiawan were said to be expendable. Oddly Japan wasn't mentioned.<BR>5. By a large majority, the "no attack on China" unless there was a direct attack on a NATO member or other ally came from outside the U.S..(Guess it is true, we do like to fight more than other Nations) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_watchout.gif"><BR>Being ex-military (4yrs USMC- 6yrs US Navy) & Viet Nam vet, I've always been interested to get other's views on incidents like this. I never trust the media or politicians to get unbiased opinions.<BR>Thanks, I enjoyed it.<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000036||SteveJ||08-14-2001||03:58 PM||||Yea, I see you trying to bow out gracfully from this thread but your not off the hook yet. Today the Chinese refused the money as insufficient.<BR>A nice slap in the face don't you think?||||reg||1
Z||000037||maryjane||08-14-2001||09:45 PM||||Bow out gracefully? Being graceful is not something I've ever been accused of. I am not at all surprised that they refused the payment, couldn't even buy a decent dumb bomb for that paltry amt. Now we can put that small payment towards SDI. I'm afraid we will need it sooner, rather than later. The old Soviet Union collapsed under the weight of not having a workable economy to fuel it's arms and space race with the west. I doubt the same will hold true with the "Peoples"(HA) Republic of China.I haven't changed my opinion in the matter of the spy plane, but if govt's are a reflection of the societies they represent (and vice-versa), then I am in the minority. I still believe a stronger reaction by the U.S. would at least have given them something to keep in the back of their mind. Hopefully, at my age, I will not be around when it comes time to say "I told ya so"||||reg||1
Z||000038||Tonker||08-14-2001||10:54 PM||||Don,<P>Thanks for sharing your findings. They are very interesting. Hopefully when it/if it comes time to say you told us so, none of us will be around. Tell ya what though. I'll owe you a beer when the ****  hits the fan.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>BTW Don, does the US Navy have the Toast of the Day, or is that only a Commonwealth Tradition?||||reg||1
Z||000039||maryjane||08-14-2001||11:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tonker:<BR><B>Don,<P>Thanks for sharing your findings. They are very interesting. Hopefully when it/if it comes time to say you told us so, none of us will be around. Tell ya what though. I'll owe you a beer when the ****  hits the fan.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>BTW Don, does the US Navy have the Toast of the Day, or is that only a Commonwealth Tradition?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If they do, it's only in the Officers Mess, I was enlisted.||||reg||1
Z||000040||Tonker||08-14-2001||11:41 PM||||Well I do know it's a wardroom tradition, I'd assumed it was in the elisted ranks as well.<BR><BR>They basically go -<BR>Sunday: Absent friends.<BR>Monday: Our ships at sea.<BR>Tuesday: Our men.<BR>Wednesday: Ourselves (the remark "since no one else is likely to think<BR>of us" often follows the toast but is not actually part of it).<BR>Thursday: A bloody war or a sickly season.<BR>Friday: A willing foe and sea room.<BR>Saturday: Sweethearts and wives ("may they never meet" is a popular<BR>response to this toast).<BR><BR>Of course once political correctness got it's ugly hands on it, some of them were changed. For example, Saturday's is now: OUR FAMILIES, and Thursday's is now: OUR NAVY.<BR><BR>God forbid traditions be kept in the Canadian Military...<p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 08-14-2001).]||||reg||1
